## §4 — What Has NOT Been Done (explicit, not silent)

**Only buildable once a real repo/CI exists — this is exactly why we're handing off now:**
- **Automated test suite.** No unit tests, no decision-table tests, no regression protection exist today. Required before any future release drops the `-rc` suffix. Priority order for test coverage: (1) `stateToGate()` / `CRITICAL_GATES` logic — this is the highest-value, highest-risk logic in the tool; (2) `ringAggregate()` / `recompute()` scoring math; (3) `pccpStatus()` combinatorial logic; (4) `standardApplies()` / date-staleness logic in `renderStandards()`; (5) applicability-matrix text generation in `applyProfile()`.
- **Immutable build identification.** The version marker currently says "commit: pending" as a static placeholder — it is not a real integrity guarantee. Once there's a build pipeline, stamp the actual commit SHA and consider a build-time SHA-256 hash of the shipped artifact, computed outside the file itself (a file cannot meaningfully attest to its own hash from inside itself).

**Deferred, not repo-blocked, genuinely still open:**
- Full 5-axis AI taxonomy split (Technology / Update Behavior / Decision Role / Deployment / Ownership) — only Model Type and Planned Modification Strategy exist today; Decision Role and Deployment axes were never added.
- Temporal Lifecycle Layer × Lifecycle Phase matrix (the review's §10) — not built. The nested-ring view remains architecture-only, not time-based.
- The two companion markdown docs (`LifecycleCompass-Spec-v2.0-2026-08-22.md`, `LifecycleCompass-Independent-Verification-Protocol-v1.0.md`) **predate the evidence-state/gate architecture and are stale.** Treat this directive and the HTML file itself as authoritative for current state; do not rely on those two docs for the current module list.
- No independent third-party regulatory review has occurred. Every verification pass to date has been a self-review, not an independent, qualified third-party check.

---

## §5 — Guardrails

### §5a — Human-committer-only policy (STRICT, non-negotiable)

- **Every commit to this repo must be authored, committed, and pushed by Rajeev Yadav personally, using his own hands on his own keyboard.** No exceptions, no phased-in tolerance, effective from the first commit.
- **No AI-authored or AI-assisted commits of any kind.** This includes but is not limited to: Claude (any surface — Code, Desktop, Cowork, chat), GitHub Copilot, Copilot Workspace, ChatGPT/Codex, Cursor, Devin, or any other AI coding agent or assistant.
- **No `Co-authored-by:` trailers naming any AI tool, model, or assistant** — not Claude, not any other model — in any commit message, ever. If a commit message template or tool default inserts one automatically, strip it before committing.
- **No bot accounts, service accounts, or automation identities as committers.** This means: no GitHub Actions bot commits to protected branches, no Dependabot auto-merge commits, no scheduled/automated commits of any kind. CI may **run** (lint, build, test) but must never itself **commit** back to the repo.
- **No AI-run or AI-triggered GitHub Actions workflows that create commits, tags, releases, or PRs on their own initiative.** A human must trigger and review every commit and every merge.
- **Enforcement**: enable and require a branch-protection rule that restricts who can push to `main` to Rajeev Yadav's account only. If GitHub's commit-signature-verification or a pre-receive hook can enforce "verified" commits tied solely to his key, use it — but the underlying rule is the identity restriction, not just the checkmark.
- **This policy governs the repo's commit history going forward**, not the pre-repo design process. The concept, ideation, and first draft of LifecycleCompass are Rajeev Yadav's own; the specification and this build were developed iteratively across roughly 8-10 different tools, of which this chat session was one. No part of that pre-repo process is characterized as AI-authored content, and the HTML file carries no such statement. Once the file is ported into the repo, every commit from that point forward is human-committed only, per §5a above.
- If an AI tool is used at any point to help draft, review, or debug code before it goes into a commit, that is a tooling choice for the human developer to make — but the **commit itself**, its authorship, and its content are the human's sole responsibility and sole signature. AI is never a co-author, never a committer, never a bot account on this repository.

### §5b — Other guardrails

- Same as every sibling Compass repo: content edits locked behind a directive process (no silent edits to the HTML), integrity/version marker embedded in the file itself, branch protection, single commit identity.
- **No PWA/Electron installer work yet** — sequencing gate: get the repo initialized, the file ported cleanly, and a minimal test suite in place first. Installer/PWA work follows the same pattern CyberCompass and RegCompass used, once this tool reaches that phase — do not start it early.
- Any change to `CRITICAL_GATES` logic, `EVIDENCE_STATES` definitions, or the standards library requires a recorded review before merge — same change-control principle as the other Compass tools' standards libraries.

